WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.294 --> 00:00:07.713
It was a very dark night, and a mist hung over the river. On London Bridge two figures emerged from the mist.

2
00:00:07.714 --> 00:00:12.555
The woman walked quickly and looked eagerly about, as if she were searching for someone.

3
00:00:12.556 --> 00:00:18.785
The boy hung back in the shadows, stopping when she stopped and picking up his pace whenever she moved on.

4
00:00:18.786 --> 00:00:27.856
The woman walked back and forth on the bridge. As the clocks chimed midnight, a young lady, accompanied by an old gentleman, emerged from a carriage.

5
00:00:27.857 --> 00:00:31.428
The woman immediately rushed up to Rose and Mr. Brownlow.

6
00:00:31.429 --> 00:00:36.674
"I'm afraid to speak to you here!" said Nancy. "Follow me down these steps."

7
00:00:36.675 --> 00:00:47.007
Hearing this, Bolter ran ahead of them. He bounded down the steps, which led to a landing overlooking the river. There he flattened himself against a pillar.

8
00:00:47.008 --> 00:00:53.841
"This is far enough," said Mr. Brownlow, stopping partway down the dark steps but still within earshot of Bolter.

9
00:00:53.842 --> 00:01:01.007
"Most people would have distrusted you too much to come this far. Why not let us speak to you on the bridge, where it is light?"

10
00:01:01.008 --> 00:01:10.920
"I'm afraid someone might see us," said Nancy. "I don't know why, but I have such a sense of dread tonight. I've been seeing signs of death all evening."

11
00:01:10.921 --> 00:01:19.721
"It's just your imagination," Mr. Brownlow said soothingly. "We waited for you on the bridge last Sunday night. Why didn't you come?"

12
00:01:19.722 --> 00:01:27.642
"I was kept away by the man that I told the lady about. I only managed to see her the first time because I slipped him a sleeping potion."

13
00:01:27.643 --> 00:01:37.308
Rose gave Nancy a sympathetic look. "Speak to her kindly," Rose said to Mr. Brownlow. "The poor creature seems to need that."

14
00:01:37.309 --> 00:01:43.134
"This young lady," he said, indicating Rose, "has told me everything that you communicated to her.

15
00:01:43.135 --> 00:01:52.697
We propose to extract the secret—whatever it may be—from this man Monks. If he won't cooperate with us, then you must hand Fagin over to us."

16
00:01:52.698 --> 00:02:02.340
"Fagin!" cried Nancy, recoiling. "Never! As evil as he is and as poorly as he has treated me, I won't turn on him."

17
00:02:02.341 --> 00:02:07.174
"Tell me why," said Mr. Brownlow, who did not seem surprised by this answer.

18
00:02:07.175 --> 00:02:15.277
"There's a band of us who have lived a bad life together for a long time. No one has ever turned on me, so I won't do that to any of them."

19
00:02:15.278 --> 00:02:20.031
"Then put Monks into my hands and let me deal with him," Mr. Brownlow urged her.

20
00:02:20.032 --> 00:02:24.341
"What if he turns against the others?" Nancy asked.

21
00:02:24.342 --> 00:02:31.341
"I promise you that if the truth is forced from him, the matter will end there. Your associates will not be harmed."

22
00:02:31.342 --> 00:02:40.428
Nancy did not respond for a few moments. Finally she turned back to Mr. Brownlow. "Monks will never find out that I'm the one who told you?"

23
00:02:40.429 --> 00:02:48.166
"Never," vowed Mr. Brownlow. "The information will be presented to him carefully, and he will never guess where it came from."

24
00:02:48.167 --> 00:02:54.341
After receiving more reassurances from both Mr. Brownlow and Rose, Nancy began to tell her story.

25
00:02:54.342 --> 00:02:59.730
She spoke so quietly that it was often difficult for Bolter to overhear what she was saying.

26
00:02:59.731 --> 00:03:05.555
After repeating the story that she had told Rose, Nancy told Mr. Brownlow where to find Monks.

27
00:03:05.556 --> 00:03:09.055
She described the pub where she had been earlier that night.

28
00:03:09.056 --> 00:03:15.460
Monks frequently went to this pub, Nancy explained, telling them when he was mostly likely to be found there.

29
00:03:15.461 --> 00:03:22.150
"He's a tall man," Nancy went on. "As he walks, he constantly looks over his shoulder.

30
00:03:22.151 --> 00:03:27.627
He looks withered and haggard even though he can't be more than 26 or 28 years old.

31
00:03:27.628 --> 00:03:35.381
His lips are often discolored and disfigured with the marks of his teeth because he has desperate fits, and sometimes he even bites his hands."

32
00:03:35.382 --> 00:03:39.357
"You must be well acquainted with Monks," said Mr. Brownlow.

33
00:03:39.358 --> 00:03:48.857
"No. I've only seen him twice, and both times he was covered up in a large, dark cloak. I've drawn out this description from others who know him.

34
00:03:48.858 --> 00:03:56.587
There's one more thing," she added. "Upon his throat and sometimes visible below his neckerchief, there is—"

35
00:03:56.588 --> 00:04:01.674
"A broad red mark like a burn or scald?" cried Mr. Brownlow.

36
00:04:01.675 --> 00:04:05.928
"Yes!" said Nancy. "Do you know him?"

37
00:04:05.929 --> 00:04:15.515
"I think I do by your description," said Mr. Brownlow. "Young woman, you've given us most valuable assistance. Now what can I do to serve you?"

38
00:04:15.516 --> 00:04:21.499
"Nothing, sir," replied Nancy, weeping. "I am past all hope indeed."

39
00:04:21.500 --> 00:04:26.753
"We could find you a safe hiding place in England or in another country if you fear to remain here.

40
00:04:26.754 --> 00:04:34.840
Before dawn you could disappear, leaving no trace. Come! I won't have you go back to your criminal associates."

41
00:04:34.841 --> 00:04:38.229
"She will be persuaded now," Rose assured him.

42
00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:42.142
But Nancy could not be persuaded to leave London and Bill Sikes.

43
00:04:42.143 --> 00:04:46.134
"I'm chained to my old life," she explained.

44
00:04:46.135 --> 00:04:57.983
"I hate it, but I cannot leave. I have gone too far to turn back." She looked around quickly. "The fear comes over me again. I must go home."

45
00:04:57.984 --> 00:05:06.634
"Home!" repeated Rose with great stress upon the word. She couldn't imagine how Nancy could think of her wretched living space as a home.

46
00:05:06.635 --> 00:05:15.872
"To such a home as I have made for myself. I hope that I have done you a service, and now all I ask is that you leave me and let me go on my way alone."

47
00:05:15.873 --> 00:05:25.523
"It's useless," said Mr. Brownlow with a sigh. "We compromise her safety by staying here. We may have already detained her longer than she expected."

48
00:05:25.524 --> 00:05:28.372
"Yes, you have," said Nancy.

49
00:05:28.373 --> 00:05:37.269
"This purse," said Rose, holding it out to Nancy. "Take it for my sake so that you may have some resources in an hour of need."

50
00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:46.682
"No, I have not done this for money. Let me console myself with that. If you want to give me something, let it be a small reminder of you."

51
00:05:46.683 --> 00:05:53.769
Rose held out a ring to Nancy, but she refused it. Rose then offered a handkerchief, which Nancy accepted.

52
00:05:53.770 --> 00:05:58.286
"Bless you, good lady," said Nancy. "Good night."

53
00:05:58.287 --> 00:06:01.334
Mr. Brownlow and Rose climbed the steps.

54
00:06:01.334 --> 00:06:11.676
As they disappeared across the bridge, Nancy sat down on the steps and vented the anguish of her heart in bitter tears. After a while she arose and ascended to the street.

55
00:06:11.677 --> 00:06:21.160
Astonished by what he had heard, Bolter remained hidden for a few more minutes. Then, glancing cautiously around, he stealthily returned to the bridge.

56
00:06:21.161 --> 00:06:28.571
Once he reached the street, Bolter ran for Fagin's house as fast as his legs could carry him.

